## 2. Exact cut force and torque

Let P be a diameter-D planar source carrying a normalized nonnegative
equilibrium stress alpha on its unit contacts and beta on its actual diameters.
For an actual cut S, orient every crossing edge from outside to inside.
Suppose all crossing contacts have one vector u, |u|=1. Put

\[
 A_S=\sum_{e\in C(S)}\alpha_e>0,\quad
 W_S=\sum_{h\in H(S)}\beta_h,
 \quad q(x)=Ju\cdot(x-o),
\]

where o is any chosen origin. For an edge e write m_e for its midpoint;
for a crossing diameter write r_h=p_{\rm in}-p_{\rm out}.

### CT.1 — force centroid identity

\[
 \sum_{h\in H(S)}\beta_h r_h=A_Su,\qquad
 A_S\bar q_S=-\sum_{h\in H(S)}\beta_h(m_h-o)\times r_h,
 \quad
 \bar q_S={\sum_{e\in C(S)}\alpha_e q(m_e)\over A_S}.       \tag{2}
\]

If every crossing diameter midpoint is within distance b of o, then

\[
 \boxed{|\bar q_S|\le {DbW_S\over A_S}.}                    \tag{3}
\]

**Proof.** Sum vertex equilibrium over S. Internal-edge forces cancel, giving
the first identity. Multiply each equilibrium equation at p by the planar
moment arm p-o and sum cross products. Internal edges again cancel, since
their forces are parallel to their endpoint differences. A crossing edge
contributes (m_e-o) cross its oriented vector: replacing the inside endpoint
by the midpoint subtracts half that vector and changes no torque. Since
(m_e-o) cross u = -q(m_e), the second identity follows. Bound each diameter
torque by |m_h-o| |r_h| <= bD to obtain (3). This uses full vector equilibrium
at actual vertices, not just projected stationarity. ∎

## 3. Threshold packet lever budget

Assume the actual transversely active gap graph is the paired tree T of the
threshold theorem. Orient every diameter from the lower A-layer to the higher
A-layer; its nonempty support is (a,b]. The unique A-parallel diameter h0 has
vector Du and support on every selected cut. Let o be the midpoint of h0,
so its transverse moment is zero.

For each simple tree edge e={a,b}, collect **all** actual rows with that
support, not just a basis or one physical representative. Write

\[
 B_e=\sum_{h\in H_e}\beta_h,\quad
 \sum_{h\in H_e}\beta_hr_h=R_eu,\quad R_e>0,
 \quad T_e=-\sum_{h\in H_e}\beta_h(m_h-o)\times r_h,
 \quad \ell_e=T_e/R_e.                                     \tag{4}
\]

The vanishing transverse resultant follows from tree-cut balance of the
strictly positive circulation. Positivity of R_e is a separately audited
geometric premise here (and follows from (L)). The axial resultant acts on
the line q=ell_e. This line is determined by its force and torque even if the
packet has many diameter bars. It need not be a source line or a contact row.

### CT.2 — additive lever and peripheral-reaction estimates

If all actual diameter midpoints are within b of o, then

\[
 \boxed{\sum_{e\in E(T)}R_e|\ell_e|
 =\sum_e|T_e|\le Db\sum_e B_e\le Db.}                       \tag{5}
\]

In particular, for t>0,

\[
 \boxed{\sum_{|\ell_e|\ge t}R_e\le Db/t.}                   \tag{6}
\]

For every selected cut i,

\[
 A_i=\beta_0D+\sum_{e:i\in(a_e,b_e]}R_e,
 \qquad A_i\bar q_i=\sum_{e:i\in(a_e,b_e]}R_e\ell_e.         \tag{7}
\]

**Proof.** The triangle inequality bounds |T_e| by Db B_e. The packets
partition the nonparallel rows of nonempty A-support, so their total beta
mass is at most one. Summing proves (5), and (6) follows by restricting the
sum to |ell_e|>=t. Sum (4) over the packets crossing cut i and include h0;
CT.1 gives (7). Rows with empty A-support do not cross these cuts and add
neither force nor torque. ∎

This additive statement bounds the reaction measure, not the count of packets
or the location of individual contact edges. With b=o(D), axial reaction at
macroscopic lever distance t=epsilon D has total size o(D).

### CT.3 — contact-force centroids away from the pivot

Let r be the common retained cut index of all intervals of T. If (L) holds,
put

\[
 k_i=|i-r|+1,\qquad \kappa_i=\min\{D/16,2k_i/3\}.
\]

Then

\[
 \boxed{A_i\ge\kappa_i W_i,\qquad
 |\bar q_i|\le Db/\kappa_i.}                               \tag{8}
\]

**Proof.** Every packet interval containing i also contains r, so its length
is at least k_i. Apply (L) to each such packet. The reference row contributes
beta_0 D >= kappa_i beta_0. The crossing mass W_i is exactly beta_0 plus
the masses of those packets. This proves the first inequality; CT.1 proves
the second. No mass is replaced by an unweighted interval count. ∎

For a canonical source, choose its full-source circumcenter c and put

\[
 \eta=3^{1/4}\sqrt D,\qquad
 b_c=\sqrt{D\eta+\eta^2}.
\]

FS.1 and the midpoint identity
|m_h-c|^2+D^2/4=(|p_h-c|^2+|q_h-c|^2)/2 give
|m_h-c|<b_c. As o is itself an actual diameter midpoint, b=2b_c suffices
for all rows. Thus b=O(D^(3/4)). For |i-r|>=epsilon D, (8) implies

\[
 |\bar q_i|\le {2b_c\over\min\{1/16,2\epsilon/3\}}
 =O_\epsilon(D^{3/4}).                                    \tag{9}
\]

This is a conditional threshold conclusion with canonical midpoint input.
It concerns the stress centroid of every such **actual** contact cut, even
when its stress mass is tiny. The sharper unpromoted midpoint theorem would
replace the exponent by 2/3 after its separate audit.

## 4. Next attack: does centroid localization localize the ports?

The answer is no under equilibrium/PSD alone. The following actual embedded
sources refute that proposed closing step. They are a specific continuation
of the repository's shell regressions, not new global-minimizer examples.

For even q>=4 let a=pi/q, R=1/(2 sin a), and take the regular unit q-gon
of radius R. Its actual contacts are its q sides and its actual diameters
are its q/2 antipodal pairs of length D=2R. Use

\[
 \alpha_e=D^2/q,\qquad \beta_h=2/q.                         \tag{10}
\]

These coefficients are strictly positive, normalized, and satisfy every
vertex equilibrium equation. The contact Laplacian acts on the coordinate
modes by 4 alpha sin^2(a)=4/q; the diameter matching acts by 2 beta=4/q.
The full scalar stress has Fourier eigenvalues

\[
 \lambda_j={4\over q}\left[
 {\sin^2(ja)\over\sin^2a}-\mathbf1_{j\ \mathrm{odd}}\right]. \tag{11}
\]

They are nonnegative, and vanish exactly at j=0,1,q-1: for odd j the smallest
nonzero sine magnitude is sin a, attained only at 1,q-1; for nonzero even
j the first term is positive. Thus Omega is PSD with exactly the affine
scalar kernel. All actual diameter midpoints are zero.

Cut off q/2 consecutive vertices. The two crossing unit sides have the
same oriented vector u. Their midpoint q-coordinates are

\[
 \pm R\cos a.
\]

They have equal positive alpha. Consequently

\[
 \boxed{\bar q_S=0,\qquad
 {1\over A_S}\sum_{C(S)}\alpha_e q(m_e)^2
 ={D^2\over4}\cos^2a.}                                    \tag{12}
\]

For any fixed 0<epsilon<1/4, all crossing contact mass lies outside
|q|<=epsilon D, although the diameter midpoint radius and the contact-force
centroid are identically zero. The contact variance is macroscopic as
D tends to infinity. When q=2 mod 4 there is also an actual diameter parallel
to u crossing the cut; the threshold reference line is therefore literal.
There is only one selected cut here, not the required linear-size complete
O--O rectangle. Its contact train stops at the bounded polygon face.

The shell has q=Theta(D), fails global optimality for large q, and for q>=6
fails canonical full mixed rigidity: its e+h=3q/2 rows have a nonzero
equilibrium dependence, so rank <=3q/2-1<2q-3. These missing hypotheses are
explicit. Formula (12) rejects centroid+full equilibrium+PSD+affine scalar
kernel as a spatial-concentration bridge; it does not reject a theorem using
the actual complete two-shore source, global optimality, and mixed rigidity.

## 5. Repaired next attack: the actual cut moment Gram

One might try to recover port concentration by applying PSD to the first
transverse moment of the cut indicator. The exact form shows why this also
supplies no upper variance bound by itself.

Let chi be the membership indicator of S on the positive-contact core.
The affine coordinate q satisfies Omega q=0. Suppose Omega is PSD. Let

\[
 G=\begin{pmatrix}
 \chi^T\Omega\chi&\chi^T\Omega(q\chi)\\
 (q\chi)^T\Omega\chi&(q\chi)^T\Omega(q\chi)
 \end{pmatrix}.
\]

### CM.1 — exact source cut moment form

\[
 \boxed{
 G=\begin{pmatrix}
 A_S-W_S& A_S\bar q_S-\sum_{H(S)}\beta_hq(m_h)\\
 A_S\bar q_S-\sum_{H(S)}\beta_hq(m_h)&
 \sum_{C(S)}\alpha_eq(m_e)^2
 -\sum_{H(S)}\beta_hq(p_{\rm out})q(p_{\rm in})
 \end{pmatrix}\succeq0.}                                  \tag{13}
\]

**Proof.** The (1,1) entry is the usual signed cut energy. The direct expansion
of the off-diagonal entry is the crossing signed sum of the inside endpoint's
q-coordinate. Summing Omega q=0 over S gives zero signed sum of crossing
q-differences. Subtract half of that identity to replace inside q by midpoint
q; parallel crossing contacts have equal endpoint q. For the final entry,
expand the signed edge energy of q chi and subtract
sum_v chi_v^2 q_v (Omega q)_v=0. Internal edges cancel; each crossing edge
leaves its signed weight times q_out q_in. Contacts again have equal q.
This proves (13), and PSD of the restricted quadratic form proves its sign. ∎

If every diameter midpoint is zero, then the off-diagonal entry is zero by
CT.1, while

\[
 G_{22}=\sum_{C(S)}\alpha_e q(m_e)^2
       +\tfrac14\sum_{H(S)}\beta_h(q(p_{\rm in})-q(p_{\rm out}))^2.
                                                               \tag{14}
\]

Both terms are nonnegative. Increasing the contact variance makes this
particular PSD condition easier to satisfy. The square and hexagon certify
this sign with exact full-source matrices. Thus the attempted repair through
this moment Gram also fails; PSD has supplied no upper contact-variance
estimate. No assertion is made that all possible source inequalities fail.

